  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 256 AM EST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\n- Dry and mild through Saturday\n\nLatest IR loop shows cirrus spilling over the Great Lakes ridge\nand moving across the cwa. Thus, we'll see muted sunshine today.\nThe upper/surface ridge remain intact across the region with short\nwaves emanating out of the Rockies trough and riding northeast\nover the ridge. As we move through the rest of the week, the upper\nridge will get shoved eastward by the Rockies trough and that will\nhelp push a cold front across the cwa Sunday. Meantime, we'll\ncontinue to see highs 15 degrees above normal. Today, highs are\nexpected in the mid to upper 50s. Thursday, around 60, Friday and\nSaturday in the lower to mid 60s (20 degrees above normal). If you\nstill have Fall yard cleanup to do, this is the week to get it\ndone.\n\n- Showers Saturday night/Sunday, cooler\n\nWe'll likely squeeze out one more dry day Saturday before ahead of\nthe cold front, but clouds will be increasing during the\nafternoon. A wave of low pressure will ride northeast along the\nfrontal boundary over the mid MS Valley and help to draw moisture\nnorthward. Models show moisture getting pulled in from the\nAtlantic and then wrapping around the bottom of the ridge before\nbeing drawn northward ahead of the front. A slug of moisture was\nnoted arriving in the southwest cwa after 06z Sunday; we'll see\nshowers develop at that point and then continue into Sunday. Model\ninstability is low and so we don't have any thunder in the\nforecast, but we'll continue to monitor. \n\nThe cold front will move through Sunday afternoon and that'll put\nan end to the balmy temperatures. Highs sunday will be in the mid\n50s and then only around 40 Monday and falling into the upper 30s\nby Wednesday.",
